<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pythonPanelDocument>
  <!-- This file contains definitions of Python interfaces and the
 interfaces menu.  It should not be hand-edited when it is being
 used by the application.  Note, that two definitions of the
 same interface or of the interfaces menu are not allowed
 in a single file. -->
  <interface name="ax_asset_library" label="AX Asset Library" icon="custom_hda_icon" showNetworkNavigationBar="false" help_url="">
    <script><![CDATA[import ax_asset_library

def createInterface():
    return ax_asset_library.create_panel()
]]></script>
    <includeInPaneTabMenu menu_position="422" create_separator="false"/>
    <help><![CDATA[= AX Asset Library =

Browse a folder library of geometry, materials, HDRIs and images, and load them into SOP, OBJ or LOP networks.

Add library folders in Settings. Each root can have a variable such as `MEGASCANS`; the panel
sets it in Houdini, so imported node paths read `$MEGASCANS/...`.
Each asset is a folder with an `asset.json` file, under a folder for its type:
`<root>/geometry|materials|hdri|images/<category>/<asset>/asset.json`.

* Double-click or press Enter to import the selected assets into the network shown in the
  network editor:
  * Geometry: SOP gets a File or Alembic SOP, OBJ a Geometry node, LOP a Reference LOP. A
    model's material is built as well and assigned: a Material SOP in SOP and OBJ, the
    Material Library LOP's assignment in LOP. When the asset has several LODs or texture
    resolutions, a dialog asks for the renderer, LOD and resolution.
  * Materials: a menu asks for Karma, Octane or Redshift. SOP and OBJ get the material in
    `/mat`, LOP gets a Material Library LOP. When the network editor shows a material network
    (`/mat`, a Material Network, or the inside of a Material Library LOP), the material is
    created there; dragging puts it at the drop point. From inside a material it goes into
    the material network above, and from any other context into `/mat`. The material is not
    assigned to anything.
  * HDRIs: OBJ gets an Environment Light, LOP a Dome Light.
  * Images: in a material network, an image becomes a texture node for the builder's renderer
    (`mtlximage` for Karma, Image texture for Octane, TextureSampler for Redshift). Elsewhere
    use __Copy path__ or drag the image onto a parameter.
  * Atlas materials import as geometry by default. A Trace SOP reads the opacity map, and each
    cutout becomes a packed primitive with UVs and the material, sized to the atlas. Choose
    __Import as material only__ for the plain material instead.
* Drag an asset onto a network editor to import it where you drop it. A material asks which
  renderer to build.
* Drag an asset onto a parameter field to paste its path. A material gives its texture folder.
* __Save selection__ writes the selected SOP node or Geometry object to the library as
  bgeo.sc (plus USD or Alembic if ticked). Saving under an existing name in the same
  category adds a new version. Pick a version in the details pane to import it, or click
  __Make current__ to make it the default.
* __Render thumbnail__ renders a Karma thumbnail with husk in the background: the model for
  geometry, a shader ball for materials, a chrome and a grey sphere for HDRIs. Renderer,
  samples and size are in Settings.
* Drop files or folders onto the panel to add them to the library:
  * Geometry files with the same name in one folder (`rock.abc`, `rock.bgeo.sc`) become one asset.
  * Texture files are grouped into materials by their channel names (`_albedo`, `_roughness`,
    `_normal`, `_height`, ...). Scan IDs like Megascans' are replaced by the folder name.
  * HDR files with a 2:1 aspect become HDRIs; other images become image assets.
  * A preview image (`rock_preview.png`, `preview.jpg`) becomes the thumbnail.
* Right-click an asset to add it to favorites, rename it (F2), move it to another category,
  or delete it (Del). Deleted assets go to the `.trash` folder in the library root; delete
  them there to free the space. Drag assets onto a category in the sidebar to move them.
  Renaming or moving relinks the nodes in the open scene; other .hip files that load the
  asset by path need relinking.
* The sidebar lists __Favorites__, __Recently used__ (your imports), __Recently added__ (last
  14 days) and __In this scene__. A green dot on a thumbnail means the asset has nodes in the
  open scene, an orange dot that some of them use an older version. Click __Update__ in the
  details pane to point them at the current version.
* Right-click a root in the sidebar to rename its display name (the folder is not renamed),
  show it in the file browser, rescan it, or remove it from the list. Right-click a category
  of a writable library to rename its folder (F2); scene nodes are relinked. Vendor
  categories can't be renamed.
* Edit an asset's tags and notes in the details pane and click __Save tags__. Favorites,
  color labels and recent imports are stored per user, not in the library.
* Dropping a Quixel Bridge download folder (usually `Downloaded`) or a Greyscalegorilla
  library folder onto the panel adds its assets to the library. Each asset's metadata gives
  its name, tags and category; the Megascans ID is appended to the name
  (`Beach_Cliff_wdeledb`). Files stay where they are unless __Copy files into the library__
  is ticked.
* A Megascans download folder can be browsed in place instead: add it in __Settings__ and set
  its __Type__ to Megascans. The panel reads Bridge's `Downloaded` folder and downloader
  folders with `3d/`, `surfaces/` and `atlases/` (name from the folder name, type from the
  parent folder). Nothing is written into that folder. Its assets are read-only; favorites
  and imports work. The first scan of a large library takes a few minutes in the background,
  later rescans a few seconds. Without preview images the thumbnails show the type letter
  until you render them; rendered thumbnails are stored in your prefs folder.
* Greyscalegorilla libraries can be browsed in place too: add the library folder in
  __Settings__ and set its __Type__ to Greyscalegorilla (it is picked automatically when the
  folder is recognized). HDRIs, materials, gobo textures (image assets) and models are read;
  names and types come from each asset's `.gsga`/`.gsgm` file. Materials use the base color,
  roughness, normal and height maps; extra maps such as sheen or anisotropy are kept with the
  asset but not wired. The GSG preview images are the thumbnails, and nothing is written into
  the library.
* Megascans FBX files are in centimeters. Imports add a Transform node with scale 0.01 so the
  asset matches the size in its metadata.
* __List__ shows assets as rows with type, category, formats and resolution. Sort by name,
  type, category, newest, recently changed or color label.
* Color labels: right-click > __Color label__, or keys 1-6 for red, orange, yellow, green,
  blue and purple; 0 clears the label. The sidebar's __Color labels__ group shows each color
  with a count, and `label:red`, `label:any` and `label:none` filter by label.
* Shortcuts in the grid: Enter imports, F2 renames, Del moves to trash, F toggles favorite,
  digits 1-6 set a color label and 0 clears it, Ctrl+C copies the path. Ctrl+F jumps to the
  search field, F5 rescans.
* Search with words or fields: `type:mat`, `tag:rock`, `cat:trees`, `fmt:abc`, `fav:1`,
  `label:red`, `-tag:wip`, `broken`.
* F5 rescans the library. Shift-click the Rescan button to clear the cache first.

After editing the code, run `ax_asset_library.dev_reload()` and reopen the panel.
]]></help>
  </interface>
</pythonPanelDocument>
